  "query_preview": "out through 8pm Saturday for the entire area. Within \nthe watch area, there will continue to be a risk of \nminor/nuisance flooding, but with a continued risk of a few \ninstances of flash flooding. Additionally, we've received some \nreports of trees coming down in the absence of much wind, and \nthis is likely due to saturated soils, not strong winds. This \nmay continue to be a risk, especially for areas that have seen \nthe most rainfall over the past 24-36 hours.\n\nLastly, right along the front there may continue to be just \nenough overlap of shear and instability to support a few weakly \nrotating updrafts with a gusty wind and weak tornado potential. \nThe risk of severe weather looks LOW, but isn't zero.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...With Sunday and Monday trending drier with the \nlingering front finally getting carried offshore, focus then \nturns to another front approaching the eastern CONUS next mid- \nweek. This boundary appears weaker and more progressive than our\ncurrent boundary but will still provide a focus for showers and\nthunderstorms. Ahead of the boundary, strengthening \nsouthwesterly flow and building low-level thicknesses will bring\na return of oppressive heat with at least a couple days of heat\nindices approaching or exceeding 105F possible.",
